  "body": {
    "id": "prop:sufficient-shortening",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(B_n/\\delta_n=o(d_n)\\), then \\(\\delta_n/s_n\\to\\infty\\), \\(L_N(C_n^*)=o(d_n)\\), and \\(L_G(C_n^*)\\lesssim d_n\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "B_n",
      "delta_n",
      "d_n",
      "s_n",
      "L_N",
      "C_star_n",
      "L_G"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:rate-scales",
      "thm:adaptive-upper-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "It isolates the constructive criterion for strict non-Gaussian shortening.",
    "gap": "Fixed-separation ACE does not state a shrinking-signal condition for honest expected-length improvement.",
    "consumer": "It gives analysts a conservative threshold for deploying the Appell inversion.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume \\(B_n/\\delta_n=o(d_n)\\), equivalently \\(\\delta_nd_n/B_n\\to\\infty\\).  Inequality (13) from the proof of the adaptive upper theorem is purely algebraic and says \\(d_ns_n\\le CB_n\\).  Since all three scales are positive,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta_n}{s_n}\n\\ge\\frac{\\delta_nd_n}{CB_n}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nThe asymptotic clause of \\(\\mathrm{thm:adaptive\\mbox{-}upper\\mbox{-}frontier}\\) is therefore applicable and gives\n\\[\nL_N(C_n^*)\\lesssim\\min\\{d_n,B_n/\\delta_n\\}=o(d_n).\n\\]\nThe same theorem gives \\(L_G(C_n^*)\\lesssim d_n\\).  This establishes all three conclusions without strengthening the law class or the nuisance-radius conditions.",
